      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking into thermal dissipation rates for a high-performance braking test. Can you compare the heat soak characteristics of carbon-ceramic pads versus semi-metallic pads during sustained high-speed deceleration? I need to understand how the thermal conductivity affects the rotor temperature over a long braking event.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For sustained high-speed deceleration, carbon-ceramic pads generally exhibit superior heat soak characteristics compared to semi-metallic pads. The thermal conductivity of carbon-carbon materials is remarkably high, allowing them to dissipate immense amounts of heat away from the pad interface much more effectively. This means that during a long braking event, the rotor temperature rise will be more controlled with carbon-ceramics, preventing the thermal degradation that can plague semi-metallics. In essence, the carbon-ceramic system manages the thermal load more gracefully, keeping temperatures lower and more stable.",
      "source": "target",
