    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for the clarification. One thing that's bothering me is the sheer unpredictability of it. It's almost like these mechanical systems are being sabotaged by something small and hidden in the system dynamics—just total, inexplicable interference that ruins the consistency of the deceleration curve. Is there any physical phenomenon, like micro-cracking or gas pocketing, that could cause that kind of \"mischievous\" or erratic friction loss?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, absolutely, that \"mischievous\" interference you're seeing is often traceable to specific physical phenomena, especially under extreme thermal cycling. Micro-cracking in the pad material or the rotor surface can expose fresh, highly reactive surfaces, leading to inconsistent friction as the material undergoes localized phase changes or wear patterns. Gas pocketing, where volatiles trapped within the pad material vaporize under intense heat, creates transient pressure fluctuations that disrupt the consistent contact pressure required for stable friction. These small, dynamic events are precisely what make high-performance braking so challenging to model perfectly.",
